// Research batch 8 — state nursing licensure and allied-health credentials.
//
// Templates covered: rn-licensure, cna-certification, pharmacy-technician, emt-certification.
//
// Deep prose is written once per template and reused across every jurisdiction that
// issues the credential. Only agency, agencyUrl, fee, note and compactMember vary.
//
// Two interstate compacts are in play in this batch and both are modelled precisely:
//
//   • nlc  — Nurse Licensure Compact (rn-licensure). Administered by NCSBN through
//            nursecompact.com. 43 jurisdictions have enacted the NLC. Of the 50 states
//            plus DC, 41 are members: 40 have fully implemented and issue multistate
//            licences today, and Massachusetts has enacted the law but has not yet set
//            an implementation date. The other two enacted jurisdictions are Guam and
//            the U.S. Virgin Islands, which are territories and therefore outside the
//            51 pages this site generates. Ten jurisdictions have NOT enacted the NLC:
//            AK, CA, DC, HI, IL, MI, MN, NV, NY, OR.
//
//   • emsc — United States EMS Compact / REPLICA (emt-certification). Administered by
//            the Interstate Commission for EMS Personnel Practice. 28 states have
//            enacted the law and the Privilege to Practice is active in 25 of them.
//            Connecticut (2026), Arizona (2026) and Alaska (2026) have enacted but are
//            not yet operational. The remaining 23 jurisdictions have not enacted.
//
// Omission policy: cna-certification and rn-licensure exist in all 51 jurisdictions, so
// no state code is omitted from those two. pharmacy-technician omits CO, DE and WI —
// those three states genuinely do not register or license pharmacy technicians at the
// state level. Hawaii and Pennsylvania, which used to belong on that omission list,
// have both enacted registration requirements (HI Act 93 of 2025, effective 1 Jan 2026;
// PA Act 140 of 2020, final regulations 28 Jun 2025 with a 28 Jun 2026 deadline) and are
// therefore included.

