    overview:
      'A registered nurse licence is the state-issued authority to practise professional nursing — assessing patients, forming a nursing diagnosis, planning and delivering care, administering medication, and directing licensed practical nurses and unlicensed assistive personnel. All 50 states and the District of Columbia issue the licence, and all of them use the same national examination: the NCLEX-RN, developed by the National Council of State Boards of Nursing (NCSBN) and delivered at Pearson VUE test centres. What actually varies between jurisdictions is the application paperwork, the fee, how fingerprints and criminal history are handled, whether jurisprudence or continuing-education modules are bolted on — and, above all, whether the state belongs to the Nurse Licensure Compact. In a compact state a nurse who declares that state as their primary state of residence can hold a single multistate licence that carries the privilege to practise, in person or by telehealth, in every other compact jurisdiction. In a non-compact state the licence is single-state only, and working anywhere else means applying for a separate licence by endorsement. That single distinction is the most consequential fact on this page, and it is the reason travel nurses and telehealth employers care so much about which side of the line a state sits on.',
    eligibility:
      'Applicants must have graduated from a nursing programme approved by a board of nursing — either an Associate Degree in Nursing (ADN, roughly two years) or a Bachelor of Science in Nursing (BSN, four years) — and the programme must be nationally accredited by ACEN, CCNE or NLN CNEA in most states. Every board runs a fingerprint-based state and FBI criminal history check, and most require the applicant to disclose any prior discipline, criminal conviction or substance-use treatment; these are reviewed case by case rather than being automatic bars. Internationally educated nurses must additionally obtain a credentials evaluation (usually a CGFNS CES Professional Report), demonstrate English proficiency through IELTS, TOEFL iBT, PTE Academic or OET unless educated in an exempt English-speaking country, and in some states hold a VisaScreen certificate. Nurses seeking a multistate licence must meet the eleven Uniform Licensure Requirements adopted by the compact states, which include US citizenship or lawful permanent residence, a valid Social Security number, and no state or federal felony conviction.',
